      "Why High-Altitude Glaciers Darken Faster Than Climate Models Predicted",
      "Climatologists have long understood that rising summer air temperatures shrink mountain glaciers, yet field glaciologists working across the European Alps, the tropical Andes, and High Mountain Asia have repeatedly watched ice sheets thin considerably faster than air-temperature curves alone would predict. A major physical culprit lies in subtle seasonal shifts in surface reflectivity, or solar albedo. Fresh winter snow reflects up to eighty-five percent of incoming shortwave solar radiation back toward space, acting as a protective thermal shield, whereas darkened summer ice absorbs sunlight and converts radiant energy directly into surface meltwater.\n\nSurprisingly, industrial factory soot and desert dust storms account for only part of that seasonal darkening. Cryosphere microbiologists drilling shallow surface cores have discovered thriving communities of cold-adapted snow and glacier ice algae that bloom across wet ice surfaces each spring. To protect their delicate photosynthetic machinery from intense high-altitude ultraviolet radiation, these microscopic organisms synthesize deep purple and crimson phenolic pigments that tint vast mountain snowfields the color of watermelon rind. Because pigmented algal cells absorb solar heat, melt tiny pockets of surrounding ice crystals into liquid water, and use that moisture alongside dissolved mineral dust to reproduce even faster, biological growth creates a powerful self-reinforcing feedback loop. Living microbes on mountain ice therefore (23).\n\nIncorporating biological albedo feedbacks into regional water-supply forecasts nonetheless presents formidable measurement hurdles. Algal blooms are spatially patchy and ephemeral, expanding rapidly after warm spring rain or Saharan dust deposition yet vanishing beneath a single summer snowfall too brief for infrequent satellite passes to separate cleanly from mineral grime. Glaciological teams are now combining hyperspectral low-altitude drone flights with portable DNA sequencing and spectral radiative-transfer models to quantify how trace nutrients, windblown dust, and melt duration govern bloom intensity. (24), catchment hydrologists can refine projections of summer river runoff for downstream irrigation districts and alpine hydroelectric dams. Understanding glacier loss ultimately requires scientists to view ice sheets not as sterile blocks of frozen water, but as (25).",
      "気候学者は気温上昇が山岳氷河を縮小させることを古くから理解してきたが、アルプスやアンデス、アジア高山帯で調査する氷河学者は、気温曲線単独の予測よりはるかに速く氷体が薄くなる現象を繰り返し目撃してきた。主因の一つは表面反射率（アルベド）の微妙な変化にある。新雪は太陽放射の最大85％を宇宙へ跳ね返す熱の盾として働くのに対し、黒ずんだ夏の氷は日光を吸収して融解水へ変えてしまう。驚くべきことに、工場の煤煙や砂漠の砂嵐はその黒ずみの要因の一部にすぎない。浅層コアを採取した微生物学者は、春ごとに湿った氷河表面で繁殖する低温適応型の雪氷藻類の群集を発見した。高地の強烈な紫外線から光合成器官を守るため、これらの微細藻類は濃い紫や紅色の色素を合成し広大な雪原をスイカの果肉色に染める。色素を持つ藻類細胞が太陽熱を吸収して周囲の氷を溶かし、生じた液体の水と溶存鉱物粉を使ってさらに増殖するため、自己強化型のフィードバックが生じる。こうして山岳氷上の生きた微生物は(23)のである。もっとも生物由来のアルベド効果を地域の水資源予測に組み込むには計測上の難題がある。藻類ブルームは斑状で短命であり、暖かい雨や砂塵飛来の後に急拡大しても夏の短い降雪で隠れるため、周期の粗い衛星観測だけでは鉱物汚れと切り分けにくい。そのため研究陣はハイパースペクトルドローン観測と携帯型DNA解析、放射伝達モデルを併用して栄養塩や融解期間と繁殖強度の関係を定量化している。(24)、水文学者は下流の灌漑地区や水力発電ダム向けに夏季河川流出量の予測精度を高められる。氷河縮小の解明には、氷体を無菌の氷塊ではなく(25)として捉える視点が欠かせない。",
